---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import HybridProto from '../islands/HybridProto';
---

<Layout title="Hybrid art test — PetPomo" noindex>
  <div class="mb-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight">Hybrid art test</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-sm" style="color: var(--ink-soft)">
      Painted 2D world, 3D pet. The background here is generated in code as a stand-in for real painted artwork — what
      is being tested is whether a 3D animal can sit convincingly inside a painting, not the painting itself.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-sm" style="color: var(--ink-soft)">
      Move the pointer across the scene for parallax. Switch the time of day and watch the light on the cat change with
      the sky. Then turn the three grounding tricks off one at a time.
    </p>
  </div>

  <HybridProto client:only="preact" />

  <section class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-5xl text-sm" style="color: var(--ink-soft)">
    <h2 class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider opacity-70">What to judge</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
      <li>Does the cat feel like it is <em>standing in</em> the scene, or pasted on top of it?</li>
      <li>Does it still hold at dawn and dusk, where the light is strongly coloured?</li>
      <li>Does the shading on the cat look like it belongs to the same world as the painted grass?</li>
      <li>Is the painted style close enough to your references to be worth pursuing?</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4">
      If the answer to the first three is yes, hybrid works and all twelve animals can be modelled against it. If it is
      no, we switch the pet to painted 2D and lose a day, not a month.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
